  data: {
    hero: {
      title: 'Knock-Out Whist',
      subtitle: 'Trick-taking',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Knock-Out Whist',
            subtitle: 'Trick-taking',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Trick-taking game where players drop out when they take no tricks. Each round has one fewer card per player (7 down to 1). Trump is turned in the first hand; thereafter the winner of the previous hand chooses trumps. Last player standing wins.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Knock-Out Whist is a simple plain-trick whist variant, often played as a children's game but with enough tactics for adult play, sometimes for small stakes. In Britain it is called Knockout Whist or Trumps; in North America it is also known as Scrounge or Rat.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two to seven players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card pack.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer deals seven cards each (first hand), clockwise. Uppermost undealt card is turned face up for trump. Subsequent hands: six cards each, then five, four, three, two, one. Winner of previous hand chooses trump after looking at their cards; on a tie, players cut for the right to choose.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Winner deals and leads: From the second deal onwards, the winner of the previous hand also deals and leads to the first trick. Some play that the dealer leads in the first deal.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Card exchange: After trumps are chosen, each player may discard 1, 2 or 3 cards and receive that many replacements (all at once). When only 2 or 1 card is dealt, only 1 or 2 can be discarded.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "No dog's life: Anyone who takes no trick is immediately out; no dog's life hand.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Options when choosing trumps: Winner may choose no trump, aces high or low, and normal or reverse ranking (low card takes).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: '2 of diamonds: The 2 of diamonds counts as permanent highest trump, above the ace of trumps.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
